    "rationale": "Evidence: all 60 development trials went off track (oval, s-bend and slalom alike), each with one line-loss event, and progress stalled at 0.04 to 0.12. Max error was about 0.061 m, just past the off-track limit of max(0.06, 0.0125 + 0.12/4) = 0.06 m. Two findings follow. (1) Sensor coverage is too narrow. The outer sensors sit at x = ±0.04 m with 12 mm footprints, so once the line centre drifts past roughly 0.058 m, every sensor reads background, the sum falls below 0.05, and the robot loses the line. The off-track limit (0.06 m) is reached at almost the same point, so the robot leaves before it can recover. (2) The rms error of about 0.03 m on every track, with low oscillation (0.07 to 0.16), points to a steady drift rather than jitter. The likely cause is the fixed left/right motor mismatch, which the weak integral term (ki 0.05) does not remove. Changes: widen the sensor array to x = -0.06, -0.03, 0, 0.03, 0.06 m so that the line stays visible out to about 0.09 m offset; enlarge the sensors to 0.016 m so the tape is seen over a wider band; raise kp from 3.0 to 4.0 for faster correction; raise ki from 0.05 to 0.3 to cancel the steady mismatch offset; raise kd from 0.3 to 0.5 to damp the stronger proportional action; lower base_speed from 1.6 to 1.4 to reduce overshoot and line loss on the tighter bends. Expected result: fewer or no line-loss events, max error well below 0.06 m, and progress beyond the 0.12 seen so far. Completion in 3 s may still be marginal at 1.4 and is the next thing to tune. Hypotheses to check: (H1) the wider array removes the line-loss-driven off-track exits; (H2) ki 0.3 reduces rms error by cancelling the mismatch bias; (H3) the robot can now finish with base_speed 1.4. Measurements wanted: completion, progress, completion time, rms and max error, line-loss events, oscillation, control effort, and controller errors. If the run still leaves the line with zero line-loss events, the drift is coming from the controller and not the sensing. If completion_time is well under 3 s, raise base_speed in steps of 0.2.",
